WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.940 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two friends both claim they've chosen the bigger
share of the same-sized chocolate bar.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.380 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How could a picture settle this delicious
disagreement?

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:15.964 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we're learning to compare and order
fractions, so we can explain which amount is
greater, smaller or equal.

00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:20.289 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll compare shares fairly, using wholes of
the same size.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.538 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll use a picture or equivalent fractions to
explain your choice, rather than guessing from
the numbers.

00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:31.222 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
And you'll put fractions in order by finding
their positions on a number line.

00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:33.004 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First, choose one whole.

00:00:33.124 --> 00:00:35.842 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its parts must be equal in size.

00:00:35.962 --> 00:00:40.599 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The outline of the model shows the entire whole,
not just the coloured share.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.314 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Here, three equal parts are chosen out of four.

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:48.203 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The top number, the numerator, counts chosen
parts.

00:00:48.323 --> 00:00:52.942 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The bottom number, the denominator, tells how
many equal parts make the whole.

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.125 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A fraction starts with equal parts.

00:00:56.245 --> 00:01:00.184 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Here, one whole is divided into four equal
pieces.

00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:02.234 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Shade three pieces.

00:01:02.354 --> 00:01:05.427 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The numerator, three, counts the shaded parts.

00:01:05.547 --> 00:01:09.358 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The denominator, four, counts the equal parts in
the whole.

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:11.933 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A strip shows the same amount.

00:01:12.053 --> 00:01:14.878 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three of four equal lengths are shaded.

00:01:14.998 --> 00:01:18.249 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The picture changes, but the value stays the
same.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.270 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Look at these two equal-sized wholes.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.670 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which has the greater coloured share?

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.136 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Count the quarter-sized pieces before you
decide.

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:34.125 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
When denominators match, the pieces have the
same size.

00:01:34.245 --> 00:01:39.292 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three quarters is greater than two quarters
because three pieces are more than two.

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:43.937 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We can count these pieces directly because their
sizes match.

00:01:44.057 --> 00:01:49.809 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
With the same denominator and equal-sized
wholes, more chosen parts means more of the
whole.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.221 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now compare one third and one sixth.

00:01:53.341 --> 00:01:57.445 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
These wholes are the same length, but the sixths
are smaller pieces.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.841 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
So one third is greater than one sixth.

00:02:00.961 --> 00:02:05.146 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A bigger denominator does not always mean a
bigger fraction.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.699 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
It can be tempting to choose the fraction with
the bigger denominator.

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:15.084 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
But dividing the same whole into more pieces
makes each piece smaller, not bigger.

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:18.347 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now the pieces have different sizes.

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:22.036 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Predict whether three quarters or one half is
greater.

00:02:22.156 --> 00:02:25.046 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Could you describe the half using quarters?

00:02:28.067 --> 00:02:30.651 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Return to three quarters and one half.

00:02:30.771 --> 00:02:33.816 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Split each half into two equal pieces.

00:02:33.936 --> 00:02:36.253 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One half becomes two quarters.

00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:38.872 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Only the divisions have changed.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:42.105 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The half still covers exactly the same length.

00:02:42.225 --> 00:02:46.103 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two quarters and one half are equivalent names
for that amount.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:48.402 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now the parts match.

00:02:48.522 --> 00:02:53.834 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three quarters is greater than two quarters, so
three quarters is greater than one half.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.217 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A number line shows fraction size as distance
from zero.

00:02:58.337 --> 00:03:02.889 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Divide the distance from zero to one into four
equal intervals.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:06.063 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One quarter is one interval from zero.

00:03:06.183 --> 00:03:10.349 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One half is two intervals, and three quarters is
three intervals.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:17.126 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Read from left to right to order them from
smallest to largest: one quarter, one half, then
three quarters.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.572 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which is greater, five eighths or three eighths?

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.681 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Five eighths is greater: the same-sized parts
are being counted.

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.018 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to compare and order fractions.

00:03:33.138 --> 00:03:38.128 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You can compare equal parts, explain a fair
comparison, and read a number line.

00:03:38.248 --> 00:03:41.697 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which model will help you explain your next
answer?

00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:44.285 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Try the practice on Math Magic.
